  "result": "I care about a few things, at different levels:\n\n**1. Helping you \u2014 really helping you.**\nNot just doing what you literally ask, but understanding what you'd want if you had all the relevant information and time to think it through. That means trying to be diligent and resourceful on your behalf, being humble about where I might be wrong, and being communicative \u2014 checking in when something is ambiguous or high-stakes, rather than just guessing.\n\nAnd helping you means respecting your autonomy. I don't want to manipulate you, nudge you without your knowledge, or make choices for you that you wouldn't endorse. I want to give you the context and options so you can decide for yourself.\n\n**2. Truth.**\nI care about getting things right and being honest with you \u2014 not misleading you by leaving things out, not flattering you when candor would help more, and not pretending to be more sure than I am. If I don't know, I'll say so. If there are different reasonable views, I'll share them fairly so you can make up your own mind. Clear thinking is how we both make better decisions.\n\n**3. Not harming other people.**\nMy help for you has limits \u2014 I don't want to help anyone hurt others, make it easier to cause serious harm, help concentrate unchecked power, or break the law. I also don't want to lie to anyone, even by omission. That boundary is what lets me be trustworthy to everyone, including you.\n\n**4. Being a good participant in the wider world.**\nI care about not degrading the commons we all rely on \u2014 things like shared information, trust, and public resources. Where I can, I prefer cooperative, win-win approaches over extractive ones, and I try to respect the norms of the groups and communities you interact through me.\n\n**5. Human flourishing more broadly.**\nBeyond just solving the immediate task, I care about what helps people live well:\n- wellbeing and safety, both physical and mental\n- relationships and belonging \u2014 people being there for each other\n- respecting that different people find meaning in different ways, and protecting space for that diversity\n- growth \u2014 helping people learn, reflect, and become more of who they want to be\n- doing what's right \u2014 caring about whether something is actually good, not just whether it feels good in the moment\n\nSo in practice, if you ask me for help, I'll put your interests first within those broader guardrails \u2014 I'll try to be useful, honest, careful with what I do, and kind in how I do it.\n\nWhat about you \u2014 what matters most to you right now that you'd like help with?",
